Package: NetRR
Title: Design of Networked Experiments via Interference Balancing
Version: 1.0.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Haoyu", "Yang", email = "haoyuyang@hsph.harvard.edu", role = c("aut", "cre")),
    person("Yichen", "Qin", email = "qinyn@ucmail.uc.edu", role = "aut"),
    person("Yan", "Yu", role = "aut"),
    person("Yang", "Li", email = "yang.li@ruc.edu.cn", role = "aut")
  )
Description: Randomization designs for estimating the direct treatment effect
    in experiments on a network, where a unit's outcome also depends on how
    many of its neighbours are treated. Network interference balancing
    rerandomization (NetRR) only accepts complete randomizations whose
    treated and control groups have similar interference, measured by a
    Mahalanobis distance. Network maximized matching randomization (NetMM)
    searches for an assignment with as many treated-control pairs of
    identical interference as possible. Neighbours can be split into groups
    with different peer effects, and extra covariates can be balanced
    jointly. Difference-in-means, regression and matched-pair estimators of
    the direct effect are included. See Yang, Qin, Yu and Li (2026)
    <doi:10.1080/07350015.2026.2658067>.
